    {
      "id": "claim_25a69b1eac1f443790ee8ce8ee94e0cc",
      "statement": "The 2026-07-06 prod-auth outage: feature 028 bound the prod Keycloak admin at 0.0.0.0:8099, which overlapped the CI app-e2e Keycloak's 127.0.0.1:8099; a leftover CI stack held the port for 6h, prod Keycloak could not bind on redeploy, and prod auth crash-looped until prod admin ports moved into the reserved range. The failed bind also rolled back keycloak-service's networking, so it came back detached from all networks and could not resolve its own Postgres (UnknownHostException) — which is why keycloak-network is now compose-managed rather than external, created and attached atomically on every `up` so Keycloak can always reach its Postgres even when the shared external networks hit the rootless re-attach race.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/runbooks/prod-reboot-resilience.md#L118-L118",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8f0f78504c2279f3fcfc20251065c4e798cd8efe6561f824d424ca10be78f758:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/runbooks/prod-reboot-resilience.md#L86-L98",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:493f5761611e29d0db0dab1eddf04456713e3ac4d1599d3972d330f8315852aa:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://infrastructure-as-code/docker/keycloak/compose.prod.yaml#L111-L120",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:42575ff7f8bbc230ffb9510923e84b8236315e4e2840d3c9281dd748d26d2b9b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://scripts/check-prod-ci-port-collision.mjs#L4-L8",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:67595cf9e09cf8dd7b742f4c2a6ac896be6ad1766773e7a5dbcf85532565230a:"
        },
